 verifier — `sumcheck_verify()` returns `SumcheckResult { challenges, final_claim }`. The oracle check is the caller's responsibility (【71†Thaler Remark 4.2†people.cs.georgetown.edu】). Removed `inner_product_sumcheck_verify`, `multilinear_sumcheck_verify`, and `coefficient_sumcheck::sumcheck_verify`.\nL200:   * Single proof type — `SumcheckProof<F>` replaces `Sumcheck<F>` and `ProductSumcheck<F>`.\nL201:   * Transcript redesigned — `send()`/`receive()`/`challenge()` replace `read()`/`write()`.\nL202:   * Legacy entry points demoted — use `runner::sumcheck()` with a prover type.\nL203:   * Wire format: EvalsInfty. `d` values per round instead of `d + 1`; consistency is now structural. Details in 【72†docs/design.md §7a】.\nL204: ### Added\nL205:   * `SumcheckProver<F>` trait — single extension point for all polynomial shapes.\nL206:   * 7 concrete provers — `MultilinearProver`, `InnerProductProver`, `CoefficientProver` (each with MSB + LSB variants), `GkrProver`.\nL207:   * `SumcheckField` trait — generic field interface; blanket impl for `ark_ff::Field` behind `feature = \"arkworks\"`.\nL208:   * `SimdRepr` trait — safe SIMD opt-in with `zerocopy` layout verification.\nL209:   * `runner::sumcheck()` — single runner with partial execution and per-round hooks.\nL210:   * Eq polynomial utilities — `eq_poly`, `eq_poly_non_binary`, O(2^v) incremental `compute_hypercube_eq_evals`.\nL211:   * Adversarial verifier tests — corrupted proofs, wrong sums, wrong final values across all prover types.\nL212:   * `no_std` support — core library works without `arkworks` feature.\nL213:   * SIMD — transparent 8-wide AVX-512 IFMA, 2-wide NEON acceleration.\nL214: ### Integrations\nL215: \nL216:   * 【73†WHIR】 (【74†PR #250】)\nL217:   * 【75†WARP】 (【76†PR #24】)\nL218: \nL219: ### Removed\nL220: \nL221:   * ~4,500 lines of legacy code — old `Prover` trait, `TimeProver`/`SpaceProver`/`BlendyProver`, `OrderStrategy`, `messages/`, `interpolation/`, `simd_ops`.\nL222: \nL223: ## [0.0.2] - 2026-02-11\nL224: ### Added\nL225:   * High-level API: `multilinear_sumcheck()` and `inner_product_sumcheck()` free functions for simple one-call sumcheck.\nL226:   * Fiat–Shamir support: `Transcript` trait with `SpongefishTranscript` (real Fiat-Shamir via 【77†SpongeFish】) and `SanityTranscript` (random challenges for testing).\nL227:   * `batched_constraint_poly` — merges dense and sparse polynomials into a single constraint polynomial.\nL228:   * Pairwise reduce for `TimeProductProver` (【78†#87】).\nL229:   * Improved order strategies (【79†#86】).\nL230:   * Pairwise compression for `TimeProver` (【80†#83】).\nL231:   * `BasicProver` for testing (【81†#84】).\nL232: ### Changed\nL233: \nL234:   * Removed `claim` from the `Prover` trait (【82†#85】).\nL235:   * Updated to criterion 0.8 (【83†#88】).\nL236: \nL237: ## [0.0.1] - 2025-08-26\nL238: ### Added\nL239: \nL240:   * Rayon parallelization for `TimeProver` and `TimeProductProver` (【84†#80】).\nL241:   * Stream iterator for sequential evaluation access (【85†#74】).\nL242:   * Benchmark improvements (【86†#75】, 【87†#76】).\nL243:   * `FileStream` for memory-mapped file-backed evaluations (【88†#67】).\nL244:   * Blendy product prover — `BlendyProductProver` (【89†#64】).\nL245:   * Refactored module structure (【90†#63】).\nL246: ### Changed\nL247: \nL248:   * Bumped arkworks dependencies from 0.4 → 0.5.\nL249:   * Avoid dynamic dispatch (`dyn`) and heap allocation (`Box`) (【91†#53】).\nL250:   * Gray code ordering for sequential Lagrange polynomial iterator (【92†#55】).\nL251: \nL252: ## [0.0.0] - 2024-02-09\nL253: ### Added\nL254:   * Initial release with three proving algorithms:\nL255:     * `SpaceProver` — quasi-linear time, logarithmic space [【93†CTY11†arxiv.org】].\nL256:     * `TimeProver` — linear time, linear space [【94†VSBW13†ieeexplore.ieee.org】].\nL257:     * `BlendyProver` — linear time, sublinear space [【95†CFFZ24†eprint.iacr.org】].\nL258:   * Product sumcheck variants (`SpaceProductProver`, `TimeProductProver`, `BlendyProductProver`).\nL259:   * `MemoryStream` for in-memory evaluation access.\nL260:   * Hypercube utilities and Lagrange polynomial interpolation.\nL261:   * Configurable reduction modes (pairwise, variablewise).\nL262:   * Benchmark suite using criterion.\nL263: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIs Sparse Matrix Reordering Effective for Sparse Matrix-Vector Multiplication? (https://arxiv.org/html/2506.10356v2)\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2506.10356v2\",\"lineno\":120}); Total lines: 460\nL83: PaToH Hypergraph Partitioning. PaToH is a multilevel hypergraph partitioning tool optimized for irregular data structures. It models multi-way relationships more effectively than standard graphs and improves data locality by minimizing cut hyperedges and balancing workload across partitions, which benefits operations like sparse matrix-vector multiplication  (【42†Çatalyürek and Aykanat, 1999】).\nL84: RCM (Reverse Cuthill–McKee) Ordering. RCM aims to reduce the bandwidth of sparse matrices. It performs a breadth-first traversal starting from a low-degree node and visits neighboring nodes in order of increasing degree. The resulting ordering is reversed, typically achieving better bandwidth reduction than the standard Cuthill–McKee method and improving memory locality during computations  (【43†Gibbs et al., 1976】; 【44†Liu and Sherman, 1976】).\nL85: Louvain Community Detection. Louvain method is a hierarchical clustering algorithm that detects communities in a graph by maximizing modularity. It first identifies small communities and then aggregates them into super-nodes, repeating the process. As a matrix reordering technique, it clusters strongly connected nodes together, which can improve performance by exploiting community structure in the graph  (【45†Blondel et al., 2008】).\nL86: ### 2.2. Experimental Setup\nL87: \nL88: To evaluate the performance impact of various reordering schemes, we conducted experiments on multiple modern CPUs using carefully controlled runtime settings. Below, we describe the hardware configurations, software environment, runtime settings, and dataset used in our experiments.\nL89: \nL90: Hardware Platforms\nL91: Our experiments were conducted on a diverse set of CPU architectures to evaluate the performance impact of reordering across different hardware configurations. The systems used are:\nL92: \nL93:   * •\nL94: \nL95: AMD-Server: AMD Ryzen Threadripper 3990X, 64 cores, 128 threads, 256 MiB L3 cache.\nL96: \nL97:   * •\nL98: \nL99: Intel-Server: Intel Core i9-10980XE @ 3.00 GHz, 18 cores, 36 threads, 24.8 MiB L3 cache.\nL100: \nL101:   * •\nL102: \nL103: Intel-Desktop: Intel Core i7-11700KF (11th Gen) @ 3.60 GHz, 8 cores, 16 threads, 16 MiB L3 cache.\nL104: \nL105:   * •\nL106: AMD-Desktop: AMD Ryzen 7 3700X, 8 cores, 16 threads, 32 MiB L3 cache.\nL107: \nL108: These systems span high-core-count servers and consumer desktop CPUs, providing a broad view of performance behaviors under different core counts, memory hierarchies, and vectorization capabilities.\nL109: \nL110: OS and Compiler\nL111: \nL112: All experiments were performed on 64-bit Linux systems using GCC 11.4 as the compiler.\nL113: \nL114: Execution Environment\nL115: To ensure reproducibility and minimize variability due to hardware-level behaviors, the following settings were applied:\nL116: \nL117:   * •\nL118: \nL119: Hyper-threading/SMT: Disabled in BIOS across all platforms to ensure threads are mapped to physical cores only.\nL120: \nL121:   * •\nL122: \nL123: Thread Pinning: Threads were pinned to physical cores using OMP_PLACES=cores and OMP_PROC_BIND=close.\nL124: \nL125:   * •\nL126: \nL127: Frequency Control: Turbo boosting was disabled in BIOS to eliminate frequency-induced performance variance.\nL128: \nL129:   * •\nL130: NUMA Binding: For NUMA systems, memory allocation was restricted to a single NUMA, ensuring memory access locality.\nL131: \nL132:   * •\nL133: Thread Counts: Experiments were run both for sequential and parallel execution. For the parallel case, experiments were run with $Cores-1$ OpenMP threads, where $Cores$ denotes the number of physical cores. Sequential execution represents SpMV being used with an outer level of parallelism, and the parallel execution represents a shared memory parallel execution of the SpMV where there is not an outer level of parallelism.\nL134: \nL135: Dataset\nL136: The evaluation utilizes a collection of structurally diverse, real-world sparse matrices from the SuiteSparse Matrix Collection (【46†Davis and Hu, 2011】; 【37†Davis, [n. d.]】). A total of 588 matrices were selected, based on two criteria:\nL137: \nL138:   * •\nL139: \nL140: Symmetric: We only used symmetric matrices from the collection because some of the reordering algorithms require it, e.g., METIS.\nL141: \nL142:   * •\nL143: Large-scale: We selected matrices containing more than 10,000 rows, eliminating small matrices because they exhibit high variance in execution time across repeated runs.\nL144: \nL145: Figure 【47†2】 presents a histogram illustrating the distribution of matrix sizes as a function of the number of rows ($M$). After filtering out matrices that are asymmetric or small, a total of 588 matrices from the SuiteSparse collection remain and are employed in the experiments.\nL146: 【48†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 2. Count of symmetric SuiteSparse matrices grouped by matrix size ($m$).\nL147: ## 3. Does Measurement Methodology Matter?\nL148: \nL149: This section examines the influence of two critical components of the benchmarking methodology: the approach to collecting repetitive timing measurements and the choice of OpenMP scheduling scheme.\nL150: ### 3.1. Approach for Repeated Measurements\nL151: In order to reduce system noise, multiple experiments are performed with each experiment being timed. Caching effects complicate the analysis of repeated computation of Sparse Matrix-Vector Multiplication (SPMV).\nL152: Even though repeated measurements are intended to reduce noise and increase the reliability of the data collected, the repeated measurements (Listing 【49†1】) often lead to inflated performance due to unnatural data reuse, particularly in parallel algorithms where subsets of input data fit entirely within local L1 or L2 caches. For example, in a simple Compressed Sparse Row (CSR) SPMV, the input vector $x$ may exhibit frequent data reuse.\nL153: Each row of $A$ accesses multiple elements of $x$, potentially causing a subset of $x$ to remain cached in local L1 or L2 memory during computation (i.e., $x$ is never invalidated or written). For some matrices the subset of X accessed by a single processor may remain resident in local caches between each measured SPMV iteration, exacerbating this effect.\nL154: However, real-world applications such as Conjugate Gradient (CG) perform additional tensor operations with a wider variation of per process access patterns requiring data to be moved between local caches. For instance, in CG (Listing 【50†3】), each iteration updates vectors used in the next iteration (e.g., the CG solution $x$, residual $r$, and direction $p$).\nL155: Specifically, the parallel write to $p$ causes a block distribution of $p$ across the processors that does not match the sparse reuse access pattern in the SpMV.\nL156: Some prior work attempts to mitigate this by flushing the cache between runs, while others, like Trotter et al. (【30†Trotter et al., 2023】), simply adopt the repeated measurement approach.\nL157: To better approximate such behavior in a controlled setting, we propose an alternative repeated measurement approach called input-output swapping (IOS), where the output vector is swapped with the input for the next iteration (Listing 【51†2】). This technique better reflects the cache reuse characteristics of real applications where the input vector cannot be cache between uses as readily, while preserving simplicity and benchmark reproducibility.\nL158: \nL159: ⬇\nL160: \nL161: for (i = 0; i < iters; i++) {\nL162: start_time = omp_get_wtime();\nL163: \nL164: csr_mv(A_CSR, m, x, y); // Perform SpMV\nL165: \nL166: end_time = omp_get_wtime();\nL167: \nL168: durations[i] = 1000 * (end_time - start_time); // Duration in ms\nL169: \nL170: }\nL171: \nL172: Listing 1: RAX: Repeated measurement of y=Ax\nL173: \nL174: ⬇\nL175: \nL176: for (i = 0; i < iters; i++) {\nL177: \nL178: start_time = omp_get_wtime();\nL179: \nL180: csr_mv(A_CSR, m, x, y);\nL181: \nL182: end_time = omp_get_wtime();\nL183: \nL184: durations[i] = 1000 * (end_time - start_time);\nL185: \nL186: temp = x; // Swap input/output\nL187: \nL188: x = y;\nL189: \nL190: y = temp;\nL191: \nL192: }\nL193: Listing 2: IOS: output becomes input to emulate real usage and disrupt cache reuse.\nL194: \nL195: ⬇\nL196: \nL197: for (iter = 0; iter < max_iter; iter++) {\nL198: \nL199: start_time = omp_get_wtime();\nL200: \nL201: csr_mv(A_CSR, m, p, Ap); // Ap = A * p\nL202: \nL203: end_time_spmv = omp_get_wtime();\nL204: \nL205: pAp = dot(p, Ap, m);\nL206: \nL207: alpha = rs_old / pAp;\nL208: #pragma omp parallel for\nL209: \nL210: for (i = 0; i < m; i++) {\nL211: \nL212: x[i] += alpha * p[i]; // Update solution\nL213: \nL214: r[i] -= alpha * Ap[i]; // Update residual\nL215: \nL216: }\nL217: \nL218: rs_new = dot(r, r, m);\nL219: \nL220: beta = rs_new / rs_old;\nL221: #pragma omp parallel for\nL222: \nL223: for (i = 0; i < m; i++) {\nL224: \nL225: p[i] = r[i] + beta * p[i]; // Update direction\nL226: \nL227: }\nL228: \nL229: rs_old = rs_new;\nL230: \nL231: }\nL232: \nL233: Listing 3: CG kernel loop highlighting realistic SPMV reuse behavior.\nL234: Figure 【52†3】 presents the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the ratio between measured GFLOPs by each benchmarking method and the GFLOPs observed in the real application, as captured using the Conjugate Gradient (CG) method. Results are aggregated across multiple machines. The plot compares two benchmarking methodologies: RAX, which conducts repeated measurements without modifying inputs, and IOS, which swaps input and output vectors to more accurately emulate real application dataflow.\n"
